FAK adapts the geometry-matched reusable-slot and completion-before-reuse
patterns to its existing expert-ring admission policy. The model consumer
selects one equal-size victim, settles prior transfers, retires the old
logical identity before overwrite, and retains failed or uncertain ownership.
It requires an explicitly enabled provider and a live execution domain.

The CUDA provider connects that consumer to checked synchronous same-allocation
refill, with bounded pinned-source staging and a fresh backend handle. It retires
the old handle before overwrite and retains failed or uncertain native ownership.
The supported arm is at most 64 MiB, byte-verbatim F32, Q2_K/Q3_K/Q4_K/Q5_K/Q6_K,
IQ2_XXS, IQ2_XS, IQ1_M, IQ4_NL or IQ4_XS, with profiling, graphs and capture
excluded. Raw-IQ metadata follows FAK's existing constructors: NewRawIQ uses
Bits=4, while the dedicated NewIQ2XXS also supports Bits=2 for the identical
raw layout. For these three lower-bit IQ formats, explicitly opted-in,
validated MemoryOffload uploads retain a private copy of that host descriptor
instead of the shared allocator's nominal-bit descriptor. Immutable weights and
opt-out uploads keep their prior behavior. Refill still requires equal source
and destination metadata; no quantization or conversion is introduced.

No upstream Python implementation, modulo layer scheduler, or graph/capture
protocol is copied here. FAK_CUDA_EXPERT_RING_REFILL=1 is required; ordinary
defaults are unchanged. Source/host controls are not CUDA build/link, physical
completion, model quality or performance qualification.
